### Assisted Search Telemetry API Testing
### Query-to-selection tracking (EP17 / C04)

### Variables
@baseUrl = http://localhost:5056/api

### ===========================================
### AUTHENTICATION (Run first to get token)
### ===========================================

### Login as Operator
### The selection endpoint checks ownership of the event, with no administrator bypass, so
### these calls must run as the same user the search event belongs to.
# @name login
POST {{baseUrl}}/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "username": "operator",
  "password": "Operator123!"
}

### ===========================================
### RECORD A SELECTION
### ===========================================

### Record the product the operator picked from a search
### The event id comes from the `searchEventId` that POST /api/ai/search returns (C15).
### Until C15 exists, take an id straight from the ProductSearchEvents table.
### Note there is no endpoint to CREATE the event: the backend writes it while serving the
### search, because only it knows the result origin, the trace id and the real latency.
POST {{baseUrl}}/ai/search-events/YOUR_SEARCH_EVENT_ID/selection
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{login.response.body.accessToken}}

{
  "productId": "YOUR_PRODUCT_ID"
}

### Expected: 204 No Content

### ===========================================
### ERROR CASES
### ===========================================

### Unknown event -> 404 Not Found
POST {{baseUrl}}/ai/search-events/00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000/selection
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{login.response.body.accessToken}}

{
  "productId": "YOUR_PRODUCT_ID"
}

### Event owned by somebody else -> 403 Forbidden
### Log in as a DIFFERENT user than the one who ran the search and repeat the first call.
### An administrator gets 403 here too: this is an ownership check, not a point-of-sale one.

### Product absent from the stored result list -> still 204
### The selection is recorded with a null rank and a warning in the log, rather than rejected:
### rejecting would leave the event looking abandoned and would corrupt the conversion KPI.
POST {{baseUrl}}/ai/search-events/YOUR_SEARCH_EVENT_ID/selection
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{login.response.body.accessToken}}

{
  "productId": "A_PRODUCT_NOT_IN_THE_RESULTS"
}

### ===========================================
### THERE IS NO READ ROUTE
### ===========================================

### By design there is no GET, no listing and no aggregation over search events.
### Analysis is done with SQL. Two examples:
###
###   -- share of searches that returned nothing, without opening the JSON
###   SELECT count(*) FILTER (WHERE "ResultsCount" = 0)::float / count(*)
###   FROM "ProductSearchEvents";
###
###   -- assisted (1) versus degraded lexical (2), with their retrieval latency
###   SELECT "SearchOrigin", count(*), percentile_cont(0.95)
###          WITHIN GROUP (ORDER BY "RetrievalMs")
###   FROM "ProductSearchEvents" GROUP BY "SearchOrigin";
